{
  // Output filename and BoardLoader data-variable name:
  //   boardName "RP2040" -> designer/boards/RP2040/RP2040.json -> const RP2040Data = {...}
  "boardName": "RP2040",

  // Fallback human-readable name shown in the designer UI.  Overridden
  // by the matching `<id>.name=` entry in a sibling-or-ancestor
  // boards.txt when one is present (matched via `<id>.build.variant=`
  // against this variant's directory name).
  "displayName": "Raspberry Pi Pico (RP2040)",

  // Same parser dialect as every other RP2040 board — see
  // ../board.json for the full rationale.
  "family": "rp2040",

  // GPIO7 (BOOT button, see chipGpios below) is a genuine bidirectional
  // GPIO merely wired to a switch to GND — not a dedicated/capacitive-only
  // pin — so it is handled entirely via the chipGpios "button" tag (full
  // capability set retained), not via inputOnlyGpios.
  "inputOnlyGpios": [],

  // Default transports — Serial only.
  "connections": ["serial"],

  "pinNotes": {},

  // pfodWeb NOTE (RP2040/RP2350 family button/LED/NeoPixel + dedicated-
  // hardware pin-exclusion audit, 2026-07-13):
  //
  // Board: Adafruit "Feather RP2040 with USB Type A Host" (product #5723)
  // — a Feather RP2040 with a native USB Type-A HOST connector (D+/D- PIO-
  // emulated USB host, plus a 5V boost-converter enable line for
  // peripheral power), a plain red status LED (D13), a single onboard
  // status NeoPixel with its own power-gate pin (GPIO21 data / GPIO20
  // power), and a BOOT button also usable as a general input (GPIO7).
  // This board's own pins_arduino.h is a bespoke (not ../generic_full/
  // common.h) pin set that already correctly omits D16/D17/D18 (USB host
  // D+/D-/5V-enable) from its D<n> alias list (pre-existing pfodWeb NOTE
  // at the top of the static-const block), but had no board.json override
  // before this audit.
  //
  // Sources consulted:
  // 1. This board's own pins_arduino.h (local file) — declares PIN_LED=13,
  //    PIN_NEOPIXEL=21, NEOPIXEL_POWER=20, PIN_BUTTON=7 ("'Boot0' button
  //    also on GPIO #7"), PIN_USB_HOST_DP=16, PIN_USB_HOST_DM=17,
  //    PIN_5V_EN=18, PIN_SPI0_MISO=8/MOSI=15/SCK=14 (general header SPI,
  //    device spi1), PIN_WIRE0_SDA=2/SCL=3 (STEMMA QT, device i2c1). D16/
  //    D17/D18 already deliberately not declared as D-aliases (pre-existing
  //    comment), but D20/D21 (NeoPixel power/data) ARE declared as plain
  //    D-aliases with no capability restriction prior to this audit.
  // 2. learn.adafruit.com/adafruit-feather-rp2040-with-usb-type-a-host/pinouts
  //    (official Adafruit guide, fetched via WebFetch). Verbatim quotes:
  //      "This little red LED is controllable in CircuitPython code using
  //      board.LED, and in Arduino as PIN_LED." (confirms GPIO13 LED)
  //      "It is connected to GPIO21" (NeoPixel data) and "there is a
  //      NeoPixel power pin on GPIO20. It needs to be set High for the
  //      NeoPixel to be powered."
  //      "The Boot button is the button on the right, located on GPIO7."
  //      "USB Host Data Plus (D+) - GPIO 16", "USB Host Data Minus (D-) -
  //      GPIO 17", "USB Host 5V Power - GPIO 18".
  //      "board.SPI() uses SCK/MO/MI pins (GPIO14, GPIO15 and GPIO8)" and,
  //      on chip-select: "there is no dedicated CS pin listed for the
  //      primary SPI connector... SPI1 CS options include: A3, D25, D13,
  //      D9" — CONFIRMS GPIO13 is merely one of several arbitrary
  //      software-selectable SS choices, not real dedicated wiring (same
  //      false-positive bus-alias pattern already fixed this session on
  //      adafruit_feather_scorpio and adafruit_feather_adalogger). This
  //      board's arduino-pico-supplied pins_arduino.h had picked that same
  //      default (PIN_SPI0_SS=13u), directly colliding with the confirmed
  //      LED pin.
  //      "This connector uses the SCL and SDA pins for I2C... the RP2040's
  //      I2C1 peripheral" (STEMMA QT, GPIO2/GPIO3) — ordinary
  //      general-purpose I2C connector pins, no override needed, matching
  //      every other audited Feather board's STEMMA QT treatment.
  //      "The 5V LED is a green LED, located directly below D6/GPIO6 next
  //      to the USB-A port. It indicates whether the USB host 5V power is
  //      enabled." — this is merely a physical-location description (the
  //      LED sits near the D6 silkscreen label); it is NOT wired to GPIO6
  //      itself (it is driven by the 5V boost converter's own status/
  //      enable circuitry). GPIO6 remains an ordinary general-purpose pin,
  //      no chipGpios entry needed.
  // 3. learn.adafruit.com/adafruit-feather-rp2040-with-usb-type-a-host/power-management
  //    (official Adafruit guide, fetched via WebFetch). Verbatim quote:
  //      "The onboard NeoPixel's power is controlled by its own GPIO pin
  //      at GPIO20. It [is] enabled by default in CircuitPython and
  //      Arduino." Also confirms no GPIO is connected to the LiPoly
  //      charge-status ("CHG") LED — that indicator is driven directly by
  //      the charger IC, not the RP2040.
  // 4. raw.githubusercontent.com/adafruit/circuitpython (main)
  //    ports/raspberrypi/boards/adafruit_feather_rp2040_usb_host/pins.c
  //    (fetched via WebFetch) — verbatim alias table: BUTTON/BOOT ->
  //    GPIO7, LED -> GPIO13, NEOPIXEL -> GPIO21, NEOPIXEL_POWER -> GPIO20,
  //    USB_HOST_DATA_PLUS -> GPIO16, USB_HOST_DATA_MINUS -> GPIO17.
  //    Independently corroborates every pin assignment in source #2 above
  //    (no separate alias found for PIN_5V_EN/GPIO18 in this particular
  //    file, but it is independently confirmed by source #2's "USB Host
  //    5V Power - GPIO 18" quote and this board's own pins_arduino.h
  //    PIN_5V_EN=18u define).
  // 5. Sibling precedents already in this repo (read for method,
  //    independently re-verified above, not copied): adafruit_feather_scorpio
  //    (near-identical PIN_LED=13 vs PIN_SPI0_SS=13 collision bug, same fix
  //    applied here), adafruit_feather_adalogger (identical collision bug
  //    AND identical "no dedicated CS pin... A3/D25/D13/D9" Adafruit quote,
  //    same board family/generation), cytron_motion_2350_pro (USB host
  //    D+/D- capabilities:[] exclusion precedent for a native USB-A host
  //    connector), adafruit_qtpy / adafruit_itsybitsy (NEOPIXEL_POWER
  //    exclusion precedent — on those boards the power-gate GPIO has NO
  //    D-alias at all, so no chipGpios entry was needed there; on THIS
  //    board GPIO20 already carries a plain D20 alias, so an explicit
  //    chipGpios restriction is needed here instead to stop it defaulting
  //    to full general-purpose capability).
  //
  // Per-pin findings and disposition:
  //   GPIO7 (PIN_BUTTON / BOOT) — TAGGED as button. Ordinary GPIO wired to
  //     a switch to GND (matches every other Adafruit RP2040 Feather BOOT
  //     button already audited this session — scorpio/adalogger/itsybitsy/
  //     qtpy all confirm this same active-low-with-internal-pull-up
  //     electrical pattern via Zephyr devicetree; no Zephyr board port
  //     exists yet for this specific USB-host variant to re-confirm
  //     independently, so this polarity is inferred from the identical
  //     sibling-board hardware design, not separately vendor-confirmed for
  //     THIS exact board — flagged here for transparency). Full
  //     digital_input/digital_output/pwm_output/button capability
  //     retained, matching the established precedent.
  //   GPIO13 (PIN_LED) — TAGGED led_high. Confirmed active-high onboard
  //     status LED via Adafruit's own pinouts guide and CircuitPython's
  //     pins.c. digital_input dropped to match this repo's established
  //     led_high precedent; pwm_output kept (analogWrite works on any
  //     RP2040 GPIO). REAL DEFECT FOUND AND FIXED: this GPIO was also the
  //     vendor's default (unused) PIN_SPI0_SS alias in pins_arduino.h,
  //     which would have contaminated this confirmed LED tag with a
  //     spurious spi_ss capability — fixed by removing `#define
  //     PIN_SPI0_SS (13u)` and its dependent `static const uint8_t SS =
  //     PIN_SPI0_SS;` line (whole file grepped afterward to confirm no
  //     dangling reference to either symbol remains).
  //   GPIO16 (PIN_USB_HOST_DP) — TAGGED capabilities:[] (excluded). Onboard
  //     native USB Type-A HOST connector D+ line (PIO-emulated USB host),
  //     confirmed via Adafruit's pinouts guide and CircuitPython's
  //     USB_HOST_DATA_PLUS alias. This board's own pins_arduino.h already
  //     omits a D16 alias, so this GPIO is currently invisible in the
  //     Designer pin picker; an explicit chipGpios entry is added anyway
  //     (matching the cytron_motion_2350_pro informativeness precedent for
  //     un-aliased dedicated-hardware GPIOs) so the exclusion is documented
  //     and guarded against a future accidental D-alias addition.
  //   GPIO17 (PIN_USB_HOST_DM) — TAGGED capabilities:[] (excluded). Same
  //     reasoning as GPIO16 (USB host D- line).
  //   GPIO18 (PIN_5V_EN) — TAGGED capabilities:[] (excluded). Onboard 5V
  //     boost-converter (TPS61023) enable line, powering the USB host
  //     connector's peripheral bus, confirmed via Adafruit's pinouts guide
  //     ("USB Host 5V Power - GPIO 18") and this board's own PIN_5V_EN
  //     alias. Same "already un-aliased, entry added for documentation"
  //     reasoning as GPIO16/17 — driving this pin as generic digital I/O
  //     would defeat the board's own USB-host power sequencing.
  //   GPIO20 (NEOPIXEL_POWER) — TAGGED capabilities:["digital_output"]
  //     only (restricted, not excluded). Onboard NeoPixel power-gate line,
  //     confirmed via Adafruit's pinouts/power-management guides ("must be
  //     set High for the NeoPixel to be powered") and CircuitPython's
  //     NEOPIXEL_POWER alias. Unlike the qtpy/itsybitsy sibling boards
  //     (where this same functional pin has NO D-alias and is therefore
  //     already invisible, needing no chipGpios entry), THIS board's
  //     pins_arduino.h already declares a plain D20 alias — without an
  //     explicit override it would default to full digital_input/
  //     digital_output/pwm_output general-purpose capability. Restricted
  //     to digital_output only: a sketch can legitimately drive this HIGH/
  //     LOW to enable/disable the onboard NeoPixel's power rail (a genuine,
  //     documented operation), but digital_input (nothing meaningful to
  //     read back) and pwm_output (a binary power-enable line, not a
  //     brightness/dimming line) are dropped.
  //   GPIO21 (PIN_NEOPIXEL) — TAGGED led_neopixel. Confirmed onboard single
  //     status NeoPixel (WS2812), output-only data line, via Adafruit's
  //     pinouts guide and CircuitPython's NEOPIXEL alias.
  //   GPIO2/GPIO3 (Wire0 SDA/SCL, STEMMA QT connector) — NOT tagged, KEEP
  //     general purpose. Confirmed ordinary general-purpose I2C connector
  //     pins via Adafruit's own pinouts guide ("This connector uses the
  //     SCL and SDA pins for I2C... the RP2040's I2C1 peripheral"),
  //     matching every other audited Feather board's STEMMA QT precedent
  //     (adafruit_feather_adalogger, adafruit_feather_scorpio).
  //   GPIO8/14/15 (general-purpose header SPI MISO/SCK/MOSI) — NOT tagged,
  //     KEEP general purpose. Confirmed genuine header-broken-out pins via
  //     Adafruit's own "board.SPI() uses SCK/MO/MI pins" pinout label, not
  //     dedicated to any onboard peripheral.
  //   GPIO6 — INVESTIGATED, NOT tagged (NO CHANGE). Adafruit's own docs
  //     describe a "5V LED" located "directly below D6/GPIO6 next to the
  //     USB-A port" indicating USB host 5V power status - but this is a
  //     physical-location description of an LED driven by the 5V boost
  //     converter's own circuitry, NOT a GPIO6-controlled indicator. No
  //     vendor source assigns GPIO6 any special role. Left at full
  //     chip-level default general-purpose capabilities.
  //   GPIO0/1 (Serial1 TX/RX), GPIO4/5/9-12/19/22-29 (A0-A3) — ordinary
  //     GPIO/UART/ADC pins, no dedicated onboard consumer confirmed. Left
  //     at chip-level defaults.
  //   BOOT/RST hardware straps — not GPIO-readable on this design (the
  //     BOOT button above is a genuine digitalRead()-able GPIO, distinct
  //     from the RP2040's QSPI-flash-CS BOOTSEL silicon trick used by the
  //     plain Raspberry Pi Pico) - out of scope, matching every other
  //     RP2040 board audited in this repo.
  //
  // Full findings/sources trail:
  // boardsDetails/rp2040/rp2040/adafruit_feather_usb_host/notes.txt
  "chipGpios": {
    "7":  { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output", "button"], "note": "PIN_BUTTON / BOOT — onboard bootloader-select pushbutton, also usable as a general input when application code is running. Confirmed via Adafruit's own pinouts guide (\"The Boot button is the button on the right, located on GPIO7\") and CircuitPython's pins.c (BUTTON/BOOT -> GPIO7). Full digital_input/digital_output/pwm_output capability retained (ordinary GPIO wired to a switch to GND, not a dedicated/capacitive-only pin), matching this repo's adafruit_feather_scorpio / adafruit_feather_adalogger / adafruit_itsybitsy PIN_BUTTON precedent." },
    "13": { "capabilities": ["digital_output", "pwm_output", "led_high"], "note": "PIN_LED — onboard status LED (D13), active-high. Confirmed via Adafruit's own pinouts guide (\"controllable in CircuitPython code using board.LED, and in Arduino as PIN_LED\") and CircuitPython's pins.c (LED -> GPIO13). digital_input dropped to match this repo's established led_high precedent for dedicated onboard LED pins; pwm_output kept since arduino-pico's analogWrite() works on any RP2040 GPIO. This GPIO was also the vendor's default (unused) PIN_SPI0_SS alias, which would have contaminated this confirmed LED tag with a spurious spi_ss capability — fixed 2026-07-13 by removing that alias (and its dependent `static const uint8_t SS` line) from pins_arduino.h, confirmed via Adafruit's own pinouts guide stating there is no dedicated CS pin on the main SPI header (GPIO13 was merely one of several arbitrary software-selectable SS choices: A3/D25/D13/D9), matching the adafruit_feather_scorpio / adafruit_feather_adalogger bus-alias-contamination fix pattern." },
    "16": { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_USB_HOST_DP — onboard native USB Type-A HOST connector D+ line (PIO-emulated USB host). Dedicated hardware, not general-purpose. Confirmed via Adafruit's own pinouts guide (\"USB Host Data Plus (D+) - GPIO 16\") and CircuitPython's pins.c (USB_HOST_DATA_PLUS -> GPIO16). This board's own pins_arduino.h already omits a D16 alias (pre-existing pfodWeb NOTE), so this GPIO is currently invisible in the Designer pin picker; this explicit entry documents the exclusion and guards against a future accidental D-alias addition, matching the cytron_motion_2350_pro USB-host-pin informativeness precedent." },
    "17": { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_USB_HOST_DM — onboard native USB Type-A HOST connector D- line. Same reasoning as GPIO16. Confirmed via Adafruit's pinouts guide (\"USB Host Data Minus (D-) - GPIO 17\") and CircuitPython's pins.c (USB_HOST_DATA_MINUS -> GPIO17)." },
    "18": { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_5V_EN — onboard 5V boost-converter (TPS61023) enable line powering the USB host connector's peripheral bus. Dedicated hardware, not general-purpose. Confirmed via Adafruit's own pinouts guide (\"USB Host 5V Power - GPIO 18\") and this board's own PIN_5V_EN=18u alias. This board's own pins_arduino.h already omits a D18 alias (pre-existing pfodWeb NOTE); this explicit entry documents the exclusion (same reasoning as GPIO16/17) — driving this pin as generic digital I/O would defeat the board's own USB-host power sequencing." },
    "20": { "capabilities": ["digital_output"], "note": "NEOPIXEL_POWER — onboard NeoPixel power-gate line, must be driven HIGH to power the onboard NeoPixel (confirmed via Adafruit's pinouts guide: \"there is a NeoPixel power pin on GPIO20. It needs to be set High for the NeoPixel to be powered\"; and power-management guide: \"enabled by default in CircuitPython and Arduino\"). Unlike this repo's adafruit_qtpy/adafruit_itsybitsy NEOPIXEL_POWER precedent (where the equivalent pin has no D-alias at all and is therefore already invisible), this board's pins_arduino.h already carries a plain D20 alias, so digital_input and pwm_output are explicitly dropped here (no meaningful read-back value, and this is a binary power-enable line rather than a brightness/dimming line) while digital_output is kept (legitimate for a sketch to enable/disable NeoPixel power)." },
    "21": { "capabilities": ["digital_output", "led_neopixel"], "note": "PIN_NEOPIXEL — onboard single status NeoPixel (WS2812), output-only data line. Power is separately gated by GPIO20 (NEOPIXEL_POWER, must be driven HIGH — see that entry). Confirmed via Adafruit's own pinouts guide (\"It is connected to GPIO21\") and CircuitPython's pins.c (NEOPIXEL -> GPIO21)." }
  },

  // Same 8-bit analogWrite() default as every other RP2040/RP2350 board.
  "pwm": { "min": 0, "max": 255, "method": "analogWrite" },

  // No hardware DAC on RP2040/RP2350.
  "dac": {},

  // RP2040's one 12-bit ADC (0-4095), ~3.3V reference — same as every
  // other RP2040 board. This board's pins_arduino.h declares no analog
  // aliases of its own (falls through to the chip-level 26-29/A0-A3
  // defaults), and this audit found no reason to override that.
  "adc": { "bits": 12, "max": 4095, "defaultRefVolts": "3.3" }
}
